e assessment typically involves a thorough examination of dietary intake, weight, body composition, and nutritional habits. It employs various techniques, including clinical, anthropometric, biochemical, and functional assessments, to establish a nutritional diagnosis. For instance, in patients with eating disorders like TCA (anorexia nervosa), the assessment focuses on dietary habits and nutritional needs, while for critically ill patients, it evaluates their overall nutritional status to guide dietary interventions.\n\nNutritional assessment is particularly important for managing patients with liver cirrhosis, as it helps identify potential deficiencies and informs treatment strategies. Additionally, it plays a vital role in monitoring the nutritional status of children to ensure proper growth and health. Tools such as the Mini Nutritional Assessment (MNA), Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool (MUST), and Short Nutritional Assessment Questionnaire (SNAQ) are often utilized in this process.\n\nOverall, nutritional assessment is essential for identifying risks of malnutrition and guiding appropriate dietary adaptations, making it a fundamental component of patient care in various medical contexts.\",97\r\n637,PREALBÚMINA,\"Prealbumin, also known as prealbúmina, is a protein that plays a crucial role in assessing nutritional status. It is particularly sensitive to acute changes in nutrition and can respond quickly to nutritional interventions, making it an important marker for evaluating a patient's nutritional condition. Prealbumin is measured both before and after treatment to monitor the effectiveness of nutritional support, especially in patients undergoing oncological treatment. This protein serves as an indicator of inflammation and infection, highlighting its relevance in clinical settings where rapid changes in nutritional status may occur. Overall, prealbumin is a valuable tool for healthcare professionals in managing and assessing the nutritional health of